  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern early this morning is defined by\nan upper trough of low pressure over west Texas and an\nanomalously strong ridge of high pressure extending from the \nGreat Basin east into the High Plains of Nebraska. Generally a \nnortheast flow has setup across Southeast Arizona this morning, \nwith a mid-level vort max spinning across central \nArizona...moving SW.\n\nIn the short term, I would expect this feature to provide \nenough support to kick off some widely scattered showers with \nembedded thunderstorms west of Tucson through daybreak. Given \nthe deeper moisture available (1.50 to 1.70 inches of PWAT in \ncentral and western Pima), I would expect generally less than a\nquarter inch of rain with localized areas up to a half inch \naccompanying the stronger thunderstorms.\n\nOtherwise, the upper-flow begins to shift from the NE to ENE \nthis afternoon, resulting in an area of modest lift/deformation\naloft moving WSW off of the ERN Mogollon Rim in NM into\nGreenlee/Graham counties and then eventually into eastern\nCochise. Drier air aloft will get entrained into the storms as \nthey move off the terrain into the Gila River Valley increasing\nthe likelihood for gusty outflow winds to 40-45 mph/blowing \ndust to impact the Gila River Valley and San Simon Valley. \nAlthough PWATs east of Tucson will be 1.30-1.50 inches, that is\nstill 125-150% of climatology. The combination of the upper\nsupport and higher moisture will result in the potential for \nheavy rainfall this afternoon/evening, especially along the\nArizona and New Mexico border. This is supported by the 19/00Z \nHREF which shows a 30-70% neighborhood probability for storms to\nproduce in excess of an inch of rain across the eastern 3\ncounties between 20/00Z and 20/06Z and a 10% probability for \nrainfall in excess of 3 inches across the San Simon Valley at \n20/03Z. The Weather Prediction Center (WPC) included the 3 \ncounties east of Tucson in the Slight Risk for flash flooding on\ntheir Day 1 Excessive Rainfall Outlook for today...and this \nfits my conceptual model. \n\nThe WPC maintains the same general area in their 'Slight' risk \nfor flash flooding in their Day 2 ERO Monday. That pesky upper-\nlow in WRN TX this morning becomes and inverted trough and begins\nto nudge into the eastern CWA Monday afternoon, providing some \ndynamics to the mix. I like the area defined, and we may need to\nconsider a Flash Flood Watch for Graham/Greenlee and Cochise \ncounties Monday...especially if today is very active along the \nAZ/NM border and the soils become saturated increasing the flash\nflood risk Monday. This feature will make it into east central\nArizona Tuesday and lift through northern Arizona Tuesday. This\nwould leave Southeast Arizona in the subsident field in its \nwake hindering the potential for widespread heavy rain across\nour neck of the woods Tuesday. In any event, the next several \ndays look to be active...especially east of Tucson.",
